88e910bd03ac52"} {"question" "q-184656" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The union of a family of indexed sets is the set containing all elements belonging to *at least one* set in the indexed family:\n\n$$\n{⋃}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}={x\\in U\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{i}\\,\\,\\text{for at least one }{A}_{i},\\,\\,i\\in I}\n$$\n\nwhere $U$ is a universal set.\n\nThe notation ${⋃}_{i=5}^{\\infty }$ means that our set of indexes is \n${5,6,7…}.$ \n\nLet's now write down a few instances of our indexed sets:\n\n- if $i=5,$ we have ${S}_{5}=[1+\\frac{1}{5},2]=[\\frac{6}{5},2]$\n\n- if $i=6,$ we have ${S}_{6}=[1+\\frac{1}{6},2]=[\\frac{7}{6},2]$\n\n- if $i=7,$ we have ${S}_{7}=[1+\\frac{1}{7},2]=[\\frac{8}{7},2]$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nand so on. \n\nNotice that as $i$ increases, \n\n- the left endpoint of the intervals approaches $1$ but never reaches it, and\n\n- the right endpoint of the intervals equals $2$ for all the sets.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{⋃}_{i=5}^{\\infty }{S}_{i}=(1,2].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038863/state.json" "file_sha256" "f66ce7d6be3334cff21aaca28820dbf192362f25e77804ff8388e910bd03ac52"} {"question" "q-184656" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038863/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "738a4e0cbcad33d280093808d8491a29f9b6bae5ddd19b897166250d0475262a"} {"question" "q-184656" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038863/state.json" "file_sha256" "f66ce7d6be3334cff21aaca28820dbf192362f25e77804ff8388e910bd03ac52"} {"question" "q-184656" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(1,2]"] ["math" "R"] ["math" "[-2,0]"] ["math" "[-2,1)"] ["math" "∅"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038863/state.json" "file_sha256" "f66ce7d6be3334cff21aaca28820dbf192362f25e77804ff8388e910bd03ac52"} {"question" "q-184656" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(1,2]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038863/state.json" "file_sha256" "f66ce7d6be3334cff21aaca28820dbf192362f25e77804ff8388e910bd03ac52"} {"question" "q-184609" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let ${X}_{i}={0,i,2i},$ then ${⋂}_{i=1}^{\\infty }{X}_{i}=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038863/state.json" "file_sha256" "f66ce7d6be3334cff21aaca28820dbf192362f25e77804ff8388e910bd03ac52"} {"question" "q-184609" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The intersection of a family of indexed sets ${{A}_{i}}_{i\\in I}$ contains all elements that belong to *all* sets in our family:\n\n$$\n{⋂}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}={x\\in U\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{i}\\,\\,\\text{for all }{A}_{i},\\,\\,i\\in I}\n$$\n\nwhere $U$ is a universal set.\n\nThe notation ${⋂}_{i=1}^{\\infty }$ means that index set is \n$N={1,2,3…}.$ \n\nLet's now write down a few instances of our indexed sets:\n\n- if $i=1,$ we have ${X}_{1}={0,1,2}$\n\n- if $i=2,$ we have ${X}_{2}={0,2,4}$\n\n- if $i=3,$ we have ${X}_{3}={0,3,6}$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nand so on. \n\nNotice that the only element that lies in all the sets is $0.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{⋂}_{i=1}^{\\infty }{X}_{i}={0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038863/state.json" "file_sha256" "f66ce7d6be