$n=2$ into the expression and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}_{2} & ={1,2,…,2(2)+1} \\\\  & ={1,2,…,5} \\\\  & ={1,2,3,4,5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038863/example-12665.json" "file_sha256" "d2fd96dc6549b476e8994e34d258b81f19a901dacd61bfbb3b4ebec69783e58a"} {"question" "e-12763" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find $\\,\\displaystyle \\bigcup_{i \\in I} S_i$, given that $I = \\{ -1, 0, 1 \\},$ and $S_i = (i-1, \\,i+1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038863/example-12763.json" "file_sha256" "62092102364635426c4b4d071229d0bdfe10c6585943fb6d6896f448c2c06cbc"} {"question" "e-12763" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The union of a family of indexed sets is the set containing all elements belonging to *at least one* set in the indexed family:\n\n$$\n\\bigcup_{i \\in I} A_i = \\big\\{ x\\in U \\: : \\: x \\in A_i \\:\\:\\textrm{for at least one } A_i,\\:\\: i \\in I \\big\\}\n$$\n\nwhere $U$ is a universal set.\n\nTherefore, in our case, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{⋃}_{i\\in I}{S}_{i} & ={S}_{-1}\\cup {S}_{0}\\cup {S}_{1} \\\\  & =(-2,0)\\,\\cup \\,(-1,1)\\,\\cup \\,(0,2) \\\\  & =(-2,2).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038863/example-12763.json" "file_sha256" "62092102364635426c4b4d071229d0bdfe10c6585943fb6d6896f448c2c06cbc"} {"question" "e-12764" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find $\\,\\displaystyle \\bigcap_{i \\in I} X_i$  given that $I = \\mathbb{N}$ and $X_i = \\{ i, \\, 2i \\}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038863/example-12764.json" "file_sha256" "8b35ed7e77dc4a3d2115c084f8b2afb401f7a412bfda70b050d4a109157f0f51"} {"question" "e-12764" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The intersection of a family of indexed sets $\\{A_i\\}_{i\\in I}$ contains all elements that belong to *all* sets in our family:\n\n$$\n\\bigcap_{i \\in I} A_i = \\big\\{ x\\in U \\: : \\: x \\in A_i \\:\\:\\textrm{for all } A_i,\\:\\: i \\in I \\big\\}\n$$\n\nwhere $U$ is a universal set.\n\nLet's now write down a few instances of our indexed sets:\n\n- if $i=1,$ we have $X_1 = \\{ 1, 2 \\}$\n\n- if $i=2,$ we have $X_2 = \\{ 2, 4 \\}$\n\n- if $i=3,$ we have $X_3 = \\{ 3, 6 \\}$\n\n- if $i=4,$ we have $X_4 = \\{ 4, 8 \\}$\n\nand so on. \n\nNotice that there is no element that belongs to all the sets. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{⋂}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}=∅.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038863/example-12764.json" "file_sha256" "8b35ed7e77dc4a3d2115c084f8b2afb401f7a412bfda70b050d4a109157f0f51"}] "usage" {"e-12764" [] "e-12763" [] "q-172665" [] "e-12665" []} "basis" 1345 "retractions" [[17592186054960 :question/problem "Let $S_{n} = {1, 2, …, n + 1}$, where $n ∈ N$. Then, $S_{4} =$"] [17592186054275 :question/problem "Find $T_2$ given that $T_n = \\{1, 2, \\ldots, 2n+1 \\}$, where $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$."] [17592186054275 :question/worked-solution "To write down the elements of $T_2$, we substitute $n=2$ into the expression and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT_{2} &= {1, 2, …, 2(2) + 1} \\\\\n&= {1, 2, …, 5} \\\\\n&= {1, 2, 3, 4, 5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186054282 :question/problem "Find $\\,\\displaystyle \\bigcup_{i \\in I} S_i$, given that $I = \\{-1, 0, 1 \\}$, and $S_i = (i-1, \\,i+1)$."] [17592186054282 :question/worked-solution "The union of a family of indexed sets is the set containing all elements belonging to *at least one* set in the indexed family:\n\n$$\n\\bigcup_{i \\in I} A_i = \\big\\{x\\in U \\:: \\: x \\in A_i \\:\\:\\textrm{for at least one} A_i,\\:\\: i \\in I \\big\\}\n$$\n\nwhere $U$ is a universal set.\n\nTherefore, in our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n⋃_(i ∈ I)S_{i} &= S_{-1}∪S_{0}∪S_{1} \\\\\n&= (-2, 0)∪(-1, 1)∪(0, 2) \\\\\n&= (-2, 2)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186054283 :question/problem "Find $\\,\\displaystyle \\bigcap_{i \\in I} X_i$ given that $I = \\mathbb{N}$ and $X_i = \\{i, \\, 2i \\}$."] [17592186054283 :question/worked-solution "The intersection of a family of indexed sets $\\{A_i\\}_{i\\in I}$ contains all elements that belong to *all* sets in our family:\n\n$$\n\\bigcap_{i \\in I} A_i = \\big\\{x\\in U \\:: \\: x \\in A_i \\:\\:\\textrm{for all} A_i,\\:\\: i \\in I \\big\\}\n$$\n\nwhere $U$ is a universal set.\n\nLet's now write down a few instances of our indexed sets:\n\n- if $i=1$, we have $X_1 = \\{1, 2 \\}$\n- if $i=2$, we have $X_2 = \\{2, 4 \\}$\n- if $i=3$, we have $X_3 = \\{3, 6 \\}$\n- if $i=4$, we have $X_4 = \\{4, 8 \\}$\n\nand so on.\n\nNotice that there is no element that belongs to all the sets. Therefore,\n\n$$\n⋂_(i ∈ I)X_{i} = ∅\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186051702 17592186051703 17592186051704 17592186051705 17592186051706] "immutable_fields" [[17592186051701 :answer-field/correct] [17592186051701 :answer-field/id] [17592186051701 :answer-field/key] [17592186051701 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
